  <h1 class="main-title">B.Tech Micro Project</h1>
  <h2 class="sub-title">Project Overview</h2>

  <h3 class="sec-title">1. Project Title - Food Waste Prediction & Kitchen Advisory System</h3>

  <h3 class="sec-title">2. Student Details</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Student Name:</b> [Student Name]</li>
    <li><b>Roll No.:</b> [Roll No.]</li>
    <li><b>Branch &amp; Section:</b> CSE / AIML / IT</li>
    <li><b>Academic Year:</b> 2026-27</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="sec-title">3. Abstract (150–200 words)</h3>
  <p>
    Institutional hostels and university canteens routinely encounter substantial food waste resulting from volatile student attendance, rigid batch cooking schedules, academic calendar variations (such as examinations and vacation breaks), and menu preference differences. This project presents an end-to-end Machine Learning-driven Food Waste Prediction and Kitchen Advisory System developed using Python and Scikit-learn. The system ingests multi-factor canteen operational data, including meal sessions (Breakfast, Lunch, Dinner), expected student headcount, dynamic menu popularity ratings, academic calendar status, and weather forecasts. Supervised ensemble regressors (Random Forest and Gradient Boosting) are trained to forecast discarded leftover food quantities in kilograms. Rather than providing passive predictions, the system implements an actionable prescriptive decision-support engine that calculates recommended preparation quantities with dynamic safety buffers, safeguarding against food shortages while curbing kitchen over-preparation. Historical analytics, real-time prediction, model explainability (feature importances), and shift data-logging are delivered through an interactive Streamlit web dashboard. The system empowers mess administrations to reduce operational expenditure, minimize carbon footprints, and transition toward sustainable, evidence-based kitchen management.
  </p>

  <h3 class="sec-title">4. Problem Statement</h3>
  <p>
    Institutional mess kitchens and college hostel canteens struggle to balance meal preparation quantities against volatile daily student demand, leading to heavy financial losses and significant quantities of preventable food waste daily. Traditional manual estimation relies on rigid batch multipliers that overlook critical variables such as dish popularity, weather conditions, examination periods, and student dining-out patterns.
  </p>

  <h3 class="sec-title">5. Objectives</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Collect and preprocess multi-dimensional canteen operational data (attendance, meal type, menu popularity, weather, academic status).</li>
    <li>Engineer informative tabular features, handling numeric scaling and categorical encoding through Scikit-learn pipelines.</li>
    <li>Train and benchmark multiple regression models (Ridge baseline, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting) to accurately predict food waste in kilograms.</li>
    <li>Implement an automated prescriptive kitchen advisory module that calculates optimal food preparation quantities with dynamic safety margins.</li>
    <li>Present analysis, prediction outputs, and risk assessments in an interactive Streamlit web interface with a continuous shift-logging feedback loop.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="sec-title">6. Technologies Used</h3>
  <table class="custom-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 10%; text-align: center;">S. No.</th>
        <th style="width: 45%;">Category</th>
        <th style="width: 45%;">Technology</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td style="text-align: center;">1</td><td>Programming Languages</td><td>Python, JavaScript (ES6+)</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="text-align: center;">2</td><td>Web Technologies (Frontend)</td><td>HTML5, CSS3, JavaScript (DOM, Fetch API), Chart.js</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="text-align: center;">3</td><td>Web Technologies (Backend)</td><td>Flask (REST API, CORS, Session Management)</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="text-align: center;">4</td><td>Authentication &amp; Security</td><td>SQLite (<code>users.db</code>), Werkzeug Password Hashing</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="text-align: center;">5</td><td>Data Processing &amp; Analysis</td><td>Pandas, NumPy</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="text-align: center;">6</td><td>Machine Learning Framework</td><td>Scikit-learn (Ridge, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting)</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="text-align: center;">7</td><td>Model Serialization</td><td>Joblib</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="text-align: center;">8</td><td>Data Visualization</td><td>Chart.js (Interactive Web), Matplotlib, Seaborn</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="text-align: center;">9</td><td>Local Data Storage</td><td>SQLite Database &amp; CSV Logs</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="text-align: center;">10</td><td>Development Environment</td><td>Visual Studio Code (VS Code)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="page-divider">Methodology and Implementation</div>

  <h3 class="sec-title">7. Existing System</h3>
  <p>
    In a conventional hostel mess workflow, kitchen managers and chief cooks estimate daily food quantities through ad-hoc rules of thumb or fixed percentage buffers over total registered students. They lack granular tracking of dish popularity, student weekend departures, or the influence of weather and exam schedules. Consequently, kitchens frequently experience massive over-preparation (resulting in bins of discarded food) or occasional food shortages. Daily logs are either maintained in paper ledgers or unanalyzed spreadsheets, preventing data-driven inventory control and repeatable planning.
  </p>

  <h3 class="sec-title">8. Proposed System</h3>
  <p>
    The proposed system provides an automated, reviewable workflow for predicting food waste and optimizing meal preparation in institutional canteens. It models complex operational interactions—such as meal session type, day of the week, weather forecasts, student token counts, and dish popularity ratings—to forecast waste in kilograms.
  </p>
  <p>
    Furthermore, it incorporates an optimization layer: rather than just warning of excess waste, it outputs a customized <b>Recommended Preparation Quantity (kg)</b> with dynamic safety buffers (5%–7%) tailored to dish popularity. An interactive Streamlit dashboard enables single-click retraining, visual trend inspections, explainable AI feature importance reviews, and daily shift feedback logging.
  </p>

  <h3 class="sec-title">9. Methodology / Workflow</h3>
  <div style="margin: 10pt 0 16pt 0;">
    <div class="workflow-step">Select or input meal parameters (Date, Meal Type, Dish, Headcount)</div>
    <div class="workflow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
    <div class="workflow-step">Collect operational context (Academic status, weather, dish popularity rating)</div>
    <div class="workflow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
    <div class="workflow-step">Preprocess data &amp; apply feature pipeline (Scaling, One-Hot Encoding via ColumnTransformer)</div>
    <div class="workflow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
    <div class="workflow-step">Predict expected food waste (kg) using trained ensemble regressor</div>
    <div class="workflow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
    <div class="workflow-step">Compute dish consumption rate adjusted for student popularity</div>
    <div class="workflow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
    <div class="workflow-step">Calculate AI-recommended preparation quantity (kg) with dynamic safety buffer</div>
    <div class="workflow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
    <div class="workflow-step">Assess waste risk level (Low, Moderate, High) and estimate cost/food savings</div>
    <div class="workflow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
    <div class="workflow-step">Display results on Streamlit dashboard and log shift actuals for continuous retraining</div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sec-title">10. Implementation with Code</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Dataset used:</b> Operational canteen logs covering 450 days (1,350 meal sessions across Breakfast, Lunch, and Dinner) incorporating registered capacity, estimated diners, actual footfall, dish popularity (1.0–5.0), portion sizes, food cooked, food consumed, and leftover waste (kg).
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Data preprocessing:</b> Features are divided into numerical (<code>dish_popularity_rating</code>, <code>estimated_diners</code>, <code>std_portion_kg</code>, <code>food_prepared_kg</code>, <code>is_weekend</code>) and categorical (<code>meal_type</code>, <code>academic_status</code>, <code>weather</code>, <code>day_of_week</code>, <code>dish_category</code>). Transformed using <code>StandardScaler</code> and <code>OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown="ignore")</code> inside a Scikit-learn <code>ColumnTransformer</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Algorithms used:</b> Ridge Regression (linear baseline), Random Forest Regressor (100 trees, max depth 12), and Gradient Boosting Regressor (120 estimators, learning rate 0.08). Evaluated using Mean Absolute Error (MAE), Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE), and Coefficient of Determination (R²).
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Software and tools:</b> Python, Streamlit, Scikit-learn, Pandas, NumPy, Matplotlib, Seaborn, Joblib, and VS Code.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Important implementation steps:</b> Preprocess features with pipelines; train candidate models and compare holdout test performance; serialize the best-performing pipeline to disk; compute recommended preparation quantities; render interactive dashboard tabs for predictions, historical analytics, explainability, and shift logging.
  </p>

  <p><b>Illustrative code using the project's analysis functions:</b></p>
  <div class="code-container">
<span style="color:#c586c0;">from</span> src.preprocess <span style="color:#c586c0;">import</span> build_preprocessor, prepare_train_test_data
<span style="color:#c586c0;">from</span> src.train <span style="color:#c586c0;">import</span> train_and_evaluate
<span style="color:#c586c0;">from</span> src.predict <span style="color:#c586c0;">import</span> FoodWastePredictor

<span style="color:#6a9955;"># 1. Train and evaluate ensemble regressors</span>
pipeline, metrics = train_and_evaluate()

<span style="color:#6a9955;"># 2. Instantiate predictor and generate decision-support recommendations</span>
predictor = FoodWastePredictor()

recommendation = predictor.recommend_kitchen_prep(
    meal_type=<span style="color:#ce9178;">"Lunch"</span>,
    academic_status=<span style="color:#ce9178;">"Regular"</span>,
    weather=<span style="color:#ce9178;">"Sunny"</span>,
    day_of_week=<span style="color:#ce9178;">"Monday"</span>,
    dish_category=<span style="color:#ce9178;">"North Indian Veg"</span>,
    dish_popularity_rating=<span style="color:#b5cea8;">4.8</span>,
    hostel_registered_count=<span style="color:#b5cea8;">650</span>,
    estimated_diners=<span style="color:#b5cea8;">510</span>,
    std_portion_kg=<span style="color:#b5cea8;">0.48</span>,
    avg_cost_per_kg=<span style="color:#b5cea8;">65.0</span>
)

<span style="color:#6a9955;"># Output summary</span>
print(<span style="color:#ce9178;">f"Predicted Leftover Waste: {recommendation['predicted_waste_kg']} kg"</span>)
print(<span style="color:#ce9178;">f"AI Recommended Prep: {recommendation['recommended_prep_kg']} kg"</span>)
print(<span style="color:#ce9178;">f"Potential Waste Saved: {recommendation['potential_waste_saved_kg']} kg"</span>)
  </div>

